---
description: Building a RustStream service without the `macros` feature (hand-written handlers, registration and main)
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Everything the attribute macros produce can be written by hand. `macros` is a convenience
  feature, not a layer: `#[subscriber]` expands into calls on the same public runtime API, so a
  service without it loses no capability that does not come *from* a declaration.
- What the attribute emits for `async fn handle(order: &Order) -> HandlerOutcome` is, in order: a
  unit struct named after the function, an `impl Handler` whose `async fn handle` carries the body,
  and the `Declared` + `SubscriberDef` impls that let `include` mount it. Every one of those traits
  is public, so **how far you write it out is a choice, not a limit**.
- So a handler is a named type implementing `Handle<In, R, O, C, S>`, whose `async fn handle(&self,
  input: &In, outs: &O, ctx: &mut Context<'_, C, S>)` is an ordinary async method. What a closure
  would capture becomes a field on that type - a closure is not a `Handle`, the body is always a
  named type.
- **Level one** - stop at the body trait and mount with
  `b.include(subscriber(source, body).build())`. There is one body trait and one constructor:
  every axis of the form matrix is a defaulted parameter the impl pins, so the signature decides
  the form and nothing else names it. `In` is the input spelling (`T` decoded, `Payload<'_>` raw
  bytes, `Message<H, P>` a decoded payload next to its typed header contract, and `[T]` /
  `[Payload<'_>]` / `[Message<H, P>]` for a page of each), `R` the reply type (`()` declares
  none), `O` the injections arena (`()` declares none), `C` the broker's typed per-delivery
  context and `S` the application state the body reads with `ctx.state()`. What the signature does
  not carry rides the chain: the declarative settings (`.name`, `.workers`, `.workers_by_key`,
  `.on_failure`, `.buffered`, `.start_at`, `.codec`), the reply wiring (`.reply()`, `.on(dest)`,
  `.publisher(policy)`), the native page cap `.batch(n)` and the document controls
  (`.describe(..)`, `.undocumented()`); `.build()` seals the chain and `include` mounts it. Nothing
  else to implement; the registration metadata is derived from the source and the body's input
  type.
- The return shape is computed from the same two axes, so an illegal combination has no type to be
  written in and each shape has exactly one accepted spelling: `Result<(), HandlerOutcome>` for a
  single message with no reply (`Ok(())` acks), `Result<Reply, HandlerOutcome>` for a single one
  with a reply, `Result<(), Vec<HandlerOutcome>>` for a page, and
  `Result<Vec<Reply>, Vec<HandlerOutcome>>` for a page with replies, whose impl header names the
  vector (`impl Handle<[Order], Vec<Confirmation>>`). There is no bare-outcome return and no
  whole-page uniform outcome; a continuation is not a shape of its own either - return
  `Err(HandlerOutcome::ack().and_after(..))`. The page vector settles element-wise and is
  page-length by contract; a mismatch panics under the `on_failure(panic = ..)` policy.
- **Level two** - implement `SubscriberDef` plus `Declared`, and `b.include(def)` works on your own
  type with the whole mount surface: the settings builder (`.name`, `.workers`, `.on_failure`,
  `.buffered`), `.publisher(..)`, `.out(marker, ..)` and `.build()`. The settings live on
  `Declared::Settings`, which must be a `SubscriberBuilder` - `SubscriberBuilder::new(def, source)`
  is public for exactly this. Implementing `IncludeDef` alone routes through the blanket
  `impl<T: IncludeDef> Declared for T`, whose `Settings = Self`: that serves the raw forms, but a
  decoded one resolves its codec through the builder, so a bare definition does not mount there -
  and the steps (`NameStep`, `WorkersStep`, `FailureStep`, `BufferedStep`) are implemented for
  `SubscriberBuilder`, not for a bare definition, so that route has no settings surface either.
- The derives are equally reproducible, and reading their expansion is the fastest way to get one
  right: `#[derive(Outgoing)]` writes `OutgoingDestination` plus a `MessageHeaders` contract
  (`NoHeaders` or `WithHeaders<H>`), `#[derive(OutSlot)]` writes `OutSlot` plus the
  `ContainsMessage` memberships, `#[derive(OutMessages)]` writes `OutMessages` over an enum's
  variants, and `#[derive(FromRef)]` writes one `FromRef` impl per field.
- The one thing genuinely lost with the feature off is the attribute surface itself: nothing reads
  a clause for you, so what the attribute would have declared you write as a trait impl.
- `use ruststream::prelude::*;` still works: the glob's macro re-exports are themselves
  feature-gated, so the rest of it comes in unchanged - the builder, `Context`, `HandlerOutcome`,
  `Name`, `Publisher`, `PublishExt`, `TypedPublisher`, and the whole manual path (the `subscriber`
  constructor, the body trait `Handle` with its input spellings `Message` and `Payload`, the
  injections arena `Outs` / `Slot` / `Publish`, the seek context `SeekContext`, plus the bare-reply
  wrapper `Bare`, `FailurePolicies` / `FailurePolicy`, `Workers`, `Unnamed`, `Out`, `Seek`,
  `DefaultSlot`, `RouterDef` and `nonzero!`).
- Mount with `b.include(subscriber(source, body).build())` (the portable form: the source resolves
  at startup), or `b.handle(subscriber, handler, metadata)` when you already hold an open
  subscriber. Routers have the same `include` method, so grouping still works.
- Write `main` yourself: `#[tokio::main]` plus `app.run().await`. The attribute's CLI (`run`,
  `asyncapi gen`) is what you give up; `run_until(..)` and `start()` are plain methods.
- State is reached through `ctx.state()`, headers through `ctx.headers()`, and publishing through a
  publisher the handler holds or reads from the state - the same objects the extractors would have
  handed over.

## A whole service, macro-free

```rust
use std::error::Error;

use ruststream::memory::MemoryBroker;
use ruststream::prelude::*;
use serde::Deserialize;

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
struct Order {
    id: u64,
}

/// The handler body. The attribute would have generated exactly this struct, named after
/// the function it decorates.
struct Receive;

impl Handle<Order> for Receive {
    async fn handle(
        &self,
        order: &Order,
        _outs: &(),
        _ctx: &mut Context<'_>,
    ) -> Result<(), HandlerOutcome> {
        tracing::info!(order.id, "processing order");
        Ok(())
    }
}

fn app() -> RustStream {
    RustStream::new(AppInfo::new("orders", "0.1.0")).with_broker(MemoryBroker::new(), |b| {
        b.include(subscriber("orders", Receive).build());
    })
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    app().run().await?;
    Ok(())
}
```

`Ok(())` acks and `Err(HandlerOutcome::retry())` nacks: the verdict is a `Result`, so the happy path
carries the reply (nothing here) and the error side carries the settlement. Post-settle work rides
that same settlement, and the return type does not change for it - return
`Err(HandlerOutcome::ack().and_after(fut))`.

The body's two unused parameters are not ceremony: `_outs` is the injections arena of a body that
declared none, and `_ctx` is the per-delivery context that is always in reach. **Keep anything that
can panic inside the future**: the dispatcher calls `handler.handle(..)` and only then wraps the
result in `catch_unwind`, so a panic raised before the first `await` escapes the guard and kills
the task instead of settling by `on_failure(panic = ..)`.

`run()` runs until `SIGINT` / `SIGTERM`; `run_until(future)` drives shutdown from a future you own
(a test signal, a timeout), and `start()` hands back a `RunningApp` for hosting the service beside
another foreground server.

## Decoding

The constructor resolves the decode codec off the mount surface, the same ladder the attribute path
uses: the scope's (`with_broker_codec`), the router chain's (`Router::with_codec`), else
`DefaultCodec`. `.codec(..)` overrides it for one registration, and `.on_failure(..)` carries the
decode-failure policy. (`typed(codec, handler)` is the wrapper underneath - it decodes the delivery
and calls the inner handler with `&T` - and the one you build yourself for the `handle` SPI below,
which reads no scope codec.)

```rust
use ruststream::codec::CborCodec;

// the decode-failure policy, the manual counterpart of `on_failure(decode = ..)`
b.include(
    subscriber("orders", Receive)
        .on_failure(FailurePolicies::default().with_decode(FailurePolicy::Retry))
        .build(),
);

// this registration's own decode codec, overriding the scope's
b.include(subscriber("audit", Receive).codec(CborCodec).build());
```

## Raw bytes

`Payload<'_>` is the raw input spelling: the wrapper derefs to `&[u8]` and borrows the delivery's
bytes, so the payload arrives undecoded and no codec is involved. This is the shape
`#[subscriber(.., raw)]` produces, and the only subscriber form available with no codec feature at
all (`[Payload<'_>]` is its page counterpart). The spelling is a wrapper rather than `[u8]` because
`[u8]` is also a page of decoded `u8` elements, and coherence cannot split the two.

```rust
struct Ingest;

impl<'p> Handle<Payload<'p>> for Ingest {
    async fn handle(
        &self,
        payload: &Payload<'p>,
        _outs: &(),
        _ctx: &mut Context<'_>,
    ) -> Result<(), HandlerOutcome> {
        parse(payload);
        Ok(())
    }
}

b.include(subscriber("frames", Ingest).build());
```

Use `b.handle(subscriber, handler, meta)` instead when the broker hands you an open subscriber
before registration (how you obtain one is broker-specific; the in-memory broker's `subscribe` is
a plain synchronous call):

```rust
use ruststream::memory::MemoryMessage;
use ruststream::runtime::HandlerMetadata;

// The SPI takes a handler over the broker's own message, not over `[u8]`.
struct Frames;

impl Handler<MemoryMessage> for Frames {
    async fn handle(&self, msg: &MemoryMessage, _ctx: &mut Context<'_>) -> HandlerOutcome {
        parse(msg.payload());
        HandlerOutcome::ack()
    }
}

let subscriber = b.broker().subscribe("orders");
b.handle(subscriber, Frames, HandlerMetadata::raw("orders"));
```

## Dependencies and state

A closure captures; a named handler holds. Anything the body needs that is fixed at registration
becomes a field, and everything the runtime owns per delivery comes off the context - there is no
`State<T>` parameter and no `FromRef` derive to make one resolve.

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};

use ruststream::codec::Codec; // the trait, for `encode`

/// Held: fixed at registration. Read off the context: owned by the runtime.
struct Confirm {
    seen: Arc<AtomicU64>,
}

struct AppState {
    receipts: MemoryPublisher,
}

// The state is the body's last axis, so the impl names it and the mount checks it against the
// app's; the reply and injection axes stay at `()` in between.
impl Handle<Order, (), (), (), AppState> for Confirm {
    async fn handle(
        &self,
        order: &Order,
        _outs: &(),
        ctx: &mut Context<'_, (), AppState>,
    ) -> Result<(), HandlerOutcome> {
        self.seen.fetch_add(order.id, Ordering::SeqCst);

        let payload = JsonCodec
            .encode(&Receipt { order_id: order.id })
            .expect("encode");
        if ctx
            .state()
            .receipts
            .raw(&payload)
            .to("receipts")
            .publish()
            .await
            .is_err()
        {
            return Err(HandlerOutcome::retry());
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

let app = RustStream::new(AppInfo::new("orders", "0.1.0"))
    .on_startup(async move |()| Ok::<_, std::convert::Infallible>(AppState { receipts }))
    .with_broker(MemoryBroker::new(), |b| {
        b.include(subscriber("orders", Confirm { seen }).build());
    });
```

`Context<'_, C, S>` names what the attribute would have inferred: `C` the broker's per-delivery
context, `S` the application state. Both default to `()`, so a handler needing neither writes
`Context<'_>`.

## Headers

`Headers<T>` is an extractor, so the manual path declares the contract on the input instead:
`Message<H, P>` is one delivery's decoded payload next to its decoded header contract, with both
public fields ready when the body runs. The pairing is structural, so two subscribers of one
subject may read different header sets, and a violation settles by the same
`on_failure(decode = ..)` policy that covers the payload.

```rust
impl Handle<Message<ChunkMeta, Chunk>> for Convert {
    async fn handle(
        &self,
        msg: &Message<ChunkMeta, Chunk>,
        _outs: &(),
        _ctx: &mut Context<'_>,
    ) -> Result<(), HandlerOutcome> {
        tracing::info!(msg.headers.task_id, "converting {}", msg.body.id);
        Ok(())
    }
}

b.include(subscriber("chunks", Convert).build());
```

A page reads `[Message<H, P>]`, one contract per element. `ctx.headers()` is still the untyped
`HeaderMap` for a discriminator read (`get_str`), `ctx.headers_mut()` is the working copy
middleware enriches, and `HeaderMap::insert_typed(&value)` writes a contract back into it.

## Publishing

`message(&value)` needs `T: OutgoingDestination`, which is what `#[derive(Outgoing)]` implements -
so without the derive the typed publish builder has nothing to resolve a destination from. Two
routes remain, and both are the ones the builder itself ends in:

```rust
use ruststream::codec::Codec; // `encode` is a trait method

// encode with the codec, then send the bytes to a destination you name
let bytes = JsonCodec.encode(&receipt)?;
publisher.raw(&bytes).to("receipts").publish().await?;

// or build the message and call the Publisher trait method directly
publisher
    .publish(OutgoingMessage::new("receipts", bytes.as_ref()).with_headers(headers))
    .await?;
```

`TypedPublisher`, `after_startup`, publish transforms and `publish_layer` are all runtime API and
work unchanged. The reply clause becomes the body's `R` axis plus the one `.reply()` on the chain -
`.on(dest)` names the destination the reply type does not declare itself, and the publisher step
decides the wire: the default and `.publisher(TypedPublisher::new(policy))` encode the reply
through a codec, while `.publisher(Bare(policy))` and `.publisher(DefaultBareReply)` send an `R` of
`Vec<u8>` as it is, the latter through the broker's default publisher. A plain unwrapped policy is
not accepted on this chain. The `Out` slot parameter becomes the `O` axis:
`Outs<(Slot<Marker, W, Enc>,)>`, where `W` is the wired live value and `Enc` the include site's
encode codec, with the capability the body needs stated as a mandatory bound on the whole entry,
one entry per marker.

```rust
impl<W, Enc> Handle<Order, (), Outs<(Slot<Primary, W, Enc>,)>> for Mirror
where
    Slot<Primary, W, Enc>: Publish,
{
    async fn handle(
        &self,
        order: &Order,
        outs: &Outs<(Slot<Primary, W, Enc>,)>,
        _ctx: &mut Context<'_>,
    ) -> Result<(), HandlerOutcome> {
        outs.get(Primary)
            .message(&Event { id: order.id })
            .to("mirror")
            .publish()
            .await
            .map_err(|_| HandlerOutcome::retry())?;
        Ok(())
    }
}

// the reply chain seals with `.build()`, and the include site binds the arena
b.include(subscriber("orders", Confirm).reply().on("confirmations").build());
b.include(subscriber("mirror", Mirror).build())
    .out(Primary, MemoryPublish)
    .build();
```

The markers come from the impl, so a missing, duplicate or extra `.out(..)` is a compile error
naming the slot, and a single unnamed slot binds through `.publisher(policy)`, which binds and
commits in one call. `outs.get(Marker)` hands back the entry itself: it keeps the `.message(..)` /
`.raw(..)` builders and is a transparent `Deref` onto the wired live value, so a broker-defined
capability is called straight on it - a body that needs one pins the concrete live type
(`Slot<Lanes, LaneRouter, Enc>`) or bounds `W` with the broker's own trait.

## Middleware

A layer is the same named-type shape one level up: `Layer<H>` wraps an inner handler, and the
wrapper implements `Handler` by delegating. This is the form the bundled layers already use, so
nothing here is macro-dependent.

```rust
struct Counted<H> {
    inner: H,
    count: Arc<AtomicU64>,
}

impl<M, C, S, H> Handler<M, C, S> for Counted<H>
where
    M: Sync,
    C: Send,
    S: Send + Sync,
    H: Handler<M, C, S>,
{
    async fn handle(&self, msg: &M, ctx: &mut Context<'_, C, S>) -> HandlerOutcome {
        self.count.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);
        self.inner.handle(msg, ctx).await
    }
}
```

## Reaching the rest: write the definition out

`b.handle` registers with the default failure policies and no settings, because it takes a handler,
not a definition. It also never reads the scope codec, so `with_broker_codec` and
`Router::with_codec` are inert on a `handle` registration - the codec is the one you named at
`typed(..)`.

A `Router` reaches a little further without a definition: `Router::workers(Workers::pool(n))` /
`Workers::keyed(n)` is the functional counterpart of the `workers(..)` clause (`BrokerScope` has no
worker control at all). The batch shapes need nothing extra - `[T]` and `[Payload<'_>]` are input
spellings of the one constructor, and both resolve the chain's codec.

Every form is reachable through that one constructor, so none is locked behind the definition
traits: the application state, the typed broker context and the injections are all axes read off
the body's own `impl Handle`, and the reply, page-cap and document steps ride the chain
(`.reply()` / `.on(..)` / `.publisher(..)`, `.batch(n)`, `.describe(..)`, `.undocumented()`).
Registrations are documented by default under `asyncapi`, which is why the
message types need `schemars::JsonSchema` and `.undocumented()` is the per-registration exit.
What is left for the definition traits is being the attribute's own expansion target, and a fully
custom definition - one whose source, metadata or settings are computed rather than named - so
implement one and the whole mount surface opens up on your own type.

```rust
use ruststream::runtime::{
    Declared, FailurePolicies, FailurePolicy, Fixed, Open, SubscriberBuilder, SubscriberDef,
    SubscriberSettings, forms,
};

// This exact case is shorter as `subscriber("orders", Receive).on_failure(..).build()`; the
// shape below is what a definition carrying its own declaration looks like. The SPI takes the
// dispatch trait `Handler`, not the manual path's `Handle`.
impl SubscriberDef for Receive {
    type Input = /* the decode kind */;
    type Context = ();
    type Handler = Self;
    type Source = Name;

    fn source(&self) -> Self::Source { Name::new("orders") }
    fn into_handler(self) -> Self { self }
}

// `Declared` is where the settings live, and its `Settings` has to be a builder. This is
// verbatim what the expansion emits: `SubscriberBuilder::new(self, source)` plus the chain the
// attribute's clauses parsed into.
impl Declared for Receive {
    type Form = forms::Subscribing;
    type Settings = SubscriberBuilder<Self, Name, (Open, Fixed, Open)>;

    fn declare(self) -> Self::Settings {
        SubscriberBuilder::new(self, Name::new("orders"))
            .on_failure(FailurePolicies::default().with_decode(FailurePolicy::Retry))
    }
}

b.include(Receive);
```

The two routes are mutually exclusive: a direct `impl Declared` conflicts with the blanket
`impl<T: IncludeDef> Declared for T`, so pick one per definition. The builder route is the one that
scales - `SubscriberBuilder` forwards `SubscriberDef`, `InjectDef`/`InjectCall`,
`PublishingDef`/`PublishingCall`, `BatchPublishingDef`/`BatchPublishingCall` and
`HasSlots`/`BindSlots`, so the same shape carries every handler form, injected slots included.
It does mean the source is written twice, in `declare()` and in the def's own `source()`, which is
what the attribute writes too.

The settings state rides in the `Settings` type (`AllOpen` when the declaration fixed none,
`Fixed` in the slot for each one it did), which is what makes naming the same setting again at the
mount site a compile error rather than a silent override. Put a setting in `declare` to keep it
next to the handler, or leave the state open and let the mount site name it - the same choice the
attribute gives you between a clause and a builder call.

Read the expansion in `crates/ruststream-macros/src/expand.rs` before writing one of these: it
names the exact `Input` kind, `Context` and form marker per handler shape, and it is the only place
the whole mapping is written down. A definition that wants the attribute's own settings surface
instead of the blanket can implement `Declared` directly and return
`SubscriberBuilder::new(self, source)` with the chain applied, which is literally what the macro
emits.

The same holds for the derives: implement `OutgoingDestination` + `MessageHeaders` for a declared
message, `OutSlot` + `ContainsMessage` for a slot marker, `OutMessages` for a set, `FromRef` per
state field. The schema hooks on `SubscriberDef` are what feed the generated AsyncAPI document, so
a hand-written definition contributes the same schemas.

Enabling `macros` remains the shorter road, and the two styles compose in one application: a
macro-free registration and an `include(def)` sit in the same `with_broker` scope.
